{
  "human": "Hey, could you whip up a C program that grabs each defined surface from a mesh as 3D coords and flattens it onto a 2D plane? The key is keeping all the distances and areas on that surface exactly the same during the transform. Just do this for every surface in the mesh, and since each one gets its own page, don't sweat it if they look like they're overlapping.",
  "ai": "Develop a C program that processes a mesh by extracting each defined surface as a set of 3D coordinates. Transform each surface individually into a 2D space while strictly preserving all internal distances and areas. Execute this operation for every surface within the mesh. Note that each surface should be treated as occupying its own distinct page; therefore, visual overlaps between surfaces in the output are acceptable and require no mitigation."
}
